NAMES.long:
Number density of oxygen impurity ions evaluated at the plasma boundary is the flux-surface-averaged particle concentration of ionized oxygen on the last closed flux surface. It is summed over all oxygen charge states from singly ionized oxygen through fully stripped oxygen, excludes neutral oxygen, and counts each ion as one oxygen particle rather than weighting it by charge.

The boundary flux-surface average is defined by

$$
\left\langle n_{\mathrm{O}} \right\rangle_b =
\lim_{\psi \to \psi_b^-}
\frac{\oint_{\psi} \left(\sum_{j=1}^{8} n_{\mathrm{O}^{j+}}\right)\, dl/B_p}
{\oint_{\psi} dl/B_p}
$$

where $\psi$ is the poloidal magnetic-flux surface label, $\psi_b$ is its value at the last closed flux surface, $n_{\mathrm{O}^{j+}}$ is the number density of oxygen ions in charge state $j$, $j=1,\ldots,8$ spans singly ionized through fully stripped oxygen, $dl$ is a poloidal contour-length element on the flux surface, $B_p$ is the magnitude of the poloidal magnetic field, and $\oint_{\psi}$ denotes integration around that flux surface. The limit is taken from the confined-plasma side when the boundary is a separatrix.

This quantity is one charge-state-summed impurity-species contribution to [flux-surface-averaged ion density at the plasma boundary](name:flux_surface_averaged_ion_density_at_plasma_boundary). It is distinct from [volume-averaged oxygen density](name:volume_averaged_oxygen_density), which averages the same charge-state-summed oxygen ion density over a confined plasma volume rather than over the boundary flux surface, and from [oxygen density at the plasma boundary], which does not carry the flux-surface-average qualifier.

Flux-surface-averaged number density of iron ions at the plasma boundary (separatrix), summed over all ion charge states from Fe$^+$ through Fe$^{26+}$ and excluding neutral iron, is the charge-state-summed iron-ion number density averaged over the boundary magnetic flux surface. The plasma boundary is the separatrix separating closed confined flux surfaces from the open-field-line region.

For an axisymmetric equilibrium, the charge-state aggregation and boundary flux-surface average are defined by:

$$
\langle n_\mathrm{Fe} \rangle_\mathrm{sep}
=
\frac{\oint_\mathrm{sep} \left(\sum_{Z_i=1}^{26} n_{\mathrm{Fe}^{Z_i+}}\right)\, d\ell / B_p}
{\oint_\mathrm{sep} d\ell / B_p}
$$

where $n_{\mathrm{Fe}^{Z_i+}}$ is the local number density of iron ions with charge number $Z_i$, $d\ell$ is an arc-length element along the separatrix contour in a poloidal plane, and $B_p$ is the poloidal magnetic-field magnitude.

This quantity is the flux-surface-averaged counterpart of [iron_density_at_plasma_boundary](name:iron_density_at_plasma_boundary); it is not a charge-state-resolved iron density and does not include neutral iron. If the separatrix contains an X-point, the value is understood as the limit of the same average over nested closed flux surfaces approaching the separatrix. The same averaging convention applies to related boundary species densities such as [flux_surface_averaged_neon_density_at_plasma_boundary](name:flux_surface_averaged_neon_density_at_plasma_boundary), [flux_surface_averaged_hydrogen_density_at_plasma_boundary](name:flux_surface_averaged_hydrogen_density_at_plasma_boundary), and [flux_surface_averaged_ion_density_at_plasma_boundary](name:flux_surface_averaged_ion_density_at_plasma_boundary).

Flux-surface-averaged toroidal nitrogen-ion velocity is the density-weighted mean of the toroidal bulk-flow velocities of nitrogen ions over the separatrix, the plasma boundary between closed and open magnetic field lines. Nitrogen ion charge states are combined as one intensive species quantity; their velocities are averaged, not summed.

The defining charge-state and flux-surface average is

$$
\langle v_{\phi,N}\rangle_{\mathrm{sep}}
=
\frac{\displaystyle\oint_{\mathcal S_{\mathrm{sep}}}
\left(\frac{\sum_{q=1}^{7} n_{N^{q+}}v_{\phi,N^{q+}}}{\sum_{q=1}^{7} n_{N^{q+}}}\right)
\frac{dS}{|\nabla\psi|}}
{\displaystyle\oint_{\mathcal S_{\mathrm{sep}}}\frac{dS}{|\nabla\psi|}}
$$

where $n_{N^{q+}}$ is the number density of nitrogen charge state $q$ in m$^{-3}$, $v_{\phi,N^{q+}}$ is its toroidal velocity component in m s$^{-1}$, $q$ ranges from 1 through 7, $dS$ is a surface-area element, and $\psi$ is a flux-surface label. The toroidal component uses the right-handed cylindrical $(R,\phi,Z)$ frame, where $R$ is major radius, $\phi$ is toroidal angle, and $Z$ is vertical height.

This quantity excludes neutral nitrogen. The related [flux-surface-averaged nitrogen density](name:flux_surface_averaged_nitrogen_density_at_plasma_boundary) is the corresponding charge-state-summed extensive quantity, while [toroidal flux-surface-averaged ion velocity](name:toroidal_flux_surface_averaged_ion_velocity_at_plasma_boundary) combines ion species rather than isolating nitrogen.

Poloidal magnetic field at a constraint position is the signed projection of the magnetic field's poloidal component at a specified constraint position onto a specified unit direction in the poloidal plane. The poloidal plane is the plane spanned by $(\hat{R},\hat{Z})$ in the right-handed cylindrical $(R,\phi,Z)$ frame, with basis $(\hat{R},\hat{\phi},\hat{Z})$ and $\hat{R}\times\hat{\phi}=\hat{Z}$, where $R$ is major radius, $\phi$ is toroidal angle, and $Z$ is vertical height; the magnetic-field component along $\hat{\phi}$ is excluded.

The projection is defined by first constructing the positive poloidal-plane direction from a nonzero direction vector at the constraint position and then taking the magnetic-field dot product:

$$
B_{\mathrm{pol},c} = \mathbf{B}(R_c,\phi_c,Z_c)\cdot\hat{\mathbf{s}}_{\mathrm{pol},c}
= B_R(R_c,\phi_c,Z_c)s_R + B_Z(R_c,\phi_c,Z_c)s_Z,\qquad
\hat{\mathbf{s}}_{\mathrm{pol},c}=\frac{\mathbf{q}_c}{|\mathbf{q}_c|}
=s_R\hat{R}+s_Z\hat{Z}
$$

where $B_{\mathrm{pol},c}$ is this signed scalar; $(R_c,\phi_c,Z_c)$ is the constraint position, with $R_c$ and $Z_c$ in meters and $\phi_c$ in radians; $\mathbf{B}$ is the magnetic-field vector in tesla; $B_R$ and $B_Z$ are its $\hat{R}$ and $\hat{Z}$ components in tesla; $\mathbf{q}_c=q_R\hat{R}+q_Z\hat{Z}$ is any nonzero vector in the poloidal plane, $|\mathbf{q}_c|$ is its Euclidean norm, and $\hat{\mathbf{s}}_{\mathrm{pol},c}$ is the resulting dimensionless positive unit direction. The coefficients $s_R$ and $s_Z$ are dimensionless, satisfy $s_R^2+s_Z^2=1$, and have no $\hat{\phi}$ component.

The constraint position identifies only the locus at which this scalar is evaluated; it does not by itself define a probe, diagnostic, optimization method, or numerical representation. This quantity is the direction-resolved counterpart of poloidal_magnetic_field; it reduces to [radial_magnetic_field](name:radial_magnetic_field) for $\hat{\mathbf{s}}_{\mathrm{pol},c}=\hat{R}$ and to [vertical_magnetic_field](name:vertical_magnetic_field) for $\hat{\mathbf{s}}_{\mathrm{pol},c}=\hat{Z}$. It differs from [parallel_magnetic_field](name:parallel_magnetic_field), whose projection direction is the magnetic-field-line unit vector, from [toroidal_magnetic_field](name:toroidal_magnetic_field), which uses the $\hat{\phi}$ component, and from [vertical_magnetic_field_at_wall](name:vertical_magnetic_field_at_wall), which fixes a wall locus and the $\hat{Z}$ component rather than a constraint-position projection direction.

Toroidal component of the nitrogen impurity ion bulk-flow velocity at the magnetic axis is the projection of the local nitrogen impurity-ion bulk flow onto the toroidal direction at the innermost confined-plasma locus where the poloidal magnetic-flux gradient vanishes. For this species-level intensive quantity, the nitrogen velocity is the density-weighted mean over all nitrogen ion charge states. The toroidal direction is $\hat{\phi}$, the unit vector for increasing toroidal angle $\phi$ in the right-handed cylindrical $(R, \phi, Z)$ frame, where $R$ is major radius, $\phi$ increases counter-clockwise when viewed from above along $+\hat{Z}$, and $Z$ is vertical height.

The charge-state-aggregated toroidal velocity is defined by the density-weighted projection of charge-state-resolved ion flows:

$$
v_{\phi,\mathrm{N},0}
=
\frac{\sum_q n_{\mathrm{N}^{q+},0}\,\left(\mathbf{v}_{\mathrm{N}^{q+},0}\cdot\hat{\phi}\right)}
{\sum_q n_{\mathrm{N}^{q+},0}}
$$

where $v_{\phi,\mathrm{N},0}$ is this on-axis nitrogen toroidal velocity, $q$ indexes nitrogen ion charge states, $n_{\mathrm{N}^{q+},0}$ is the local density of charge state $q$ at the magnetic axis, $\mathbf{v}_{\mathrm{N}^{q+},0}$ is its bulk-flow velocity at the magnetic axis, and the subscript $0$ denotes the magnetic-axis value. The denominator is the charge-state sum that forms [nitrogen density at the magnetic axis](name:nitrogen_density_at_magnetic_axis).

This is a local on-axis species-level velocity, not a flux-surface-averaged quantity and not a charge-state-summed velocity. It is defined only where the charge-state-summed nitrogen ion density at the magnetic axis is nonzero. It is the nitrogen counterpart, at the same locus, of [toroidal deuterium velocity at the magnetic axis](name:toroidal_deuterium_velocity_at_magnetic_axis), and it is distinguished from nitrogen toroidal velocity at other radial loci such as [the pedestal top](name:toroidal_nitrogen_velocity_at_pedestal_top) or [an internal transport barrier](name:toroidal_nitrogen_velocity_at_internal_transport_barrier).
